# CLAUDE.md

## Overview

{{DESCRIPTION}}

Authenticated via Google SSO OAuth 2.1 — only `@{{GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_DOMAIN}}` Workspace accounts can connect (enforced by Google's "Internal" consent screen).

## Commands

```bash
# Development
bun run dev          # Start local dev server (wrangler dev)

# Deployment
bun run deploy       # Deploy to Cloudflare Workers

# Logs
bun run logs         # Stream live logs (wrangler tail --format pretty)
bun run tail         # Raw tail output

# Secrets
bun run secrets      # List configured secrets
wrangler secret put SECRET_NAME  # Set a secret

# KV namespace (one-time setup)
wrangler kv namespace create "OAUTH_KV"  # Paste ID into wrangler.toml
```

## Required Secrets

Set via `wrangler secret put`:
- `{{SERVICE_NAME_UPPER}}_API_KEY` — API key for the service
- `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` — Google OAuth 2.0 client ID (from Google Cloud Console)
- `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` — Google OAuth 2.0 client secret
- `COOKIE_ENCRYPTION_KEY` — generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`

## Architecture

OAuth-protected MCP server using Cloudflare Workers with Durable Objects.

```
Claude Desktop → /mcp → OAuthProvider returns 401
  → Claude opens browser → /authorize → approval dialog → Google sign-in
  → /callback → completes OAuth → Claude stores token
  → /mcp requests now authenticated
```

### Key Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `src/index.ts` | `{{SERVICE_NAME_CLASS}}MCP` McpAgent class (Durable Object) + OAuthProvider config |
| `src/google-handler.ts` | Hono app handling Google OAuth flow (/authorize, /callback) |
| `src/utils.ts` | Google OAuth helpers (authorize URL, token exchange, types) |
| `src/workers-oauth-utils.ts` | CSRF/state management, approval dialog (from Cloudflare reference) |

### Tool Pattern

All tools follow the same pattern:
1. Registered in `{{SERVICE_NAME_CLASS}}MCP.init()` via `this.server.registerTool()` with Zod input schemas
2. Structured logging: `console.log(JSON.stringify({ tool, ...params, timestamp }))`
3. Call `client.request<T>(endpoint)` for authenticated API access
4. Truncate large responses with `truncateResponse()`

